  "behavior": [
    "Original account exposes a host-only native credential handle and passive nonsecret account snapshot. Both read the original grant through the captured credentials service, rechecking the original lifetime after both async boundaries.",
    "Credential authority includes original service identity, closed/removal state and actual grant lifetime; profile-cache refresh does not revoke it. Passive cached identity has its own profile object/ID currentness and remains null until actually observed.",
    "Legacy token eligibility is preserved; native capture additionally requires the exact configured issuer. Missing or malformed native APIs never fall back to legacy token lookup.",
    "Account provider captures the actual account and API, root admission, token/signal/check fields; strict synchronous checks run before and after the captured witness callback.",
    "The original 401 handler settles with the captured original account even after service replacement. Local cleanup failure does not replace the original provider failure.",
    "Native Messages combines account cancellation with the original request. Final account check is followed by the original native write permit check before fetch.",
    "Account withdrawal wrapped by extension preparation becomes terminal ABORTED. Actual classified HTTP failures retain status and original 401 settlement. HTTP success still accepts the original extension contribution after account withdrawal.",
    "The original catalog retains its credential witness with the existing scope, retires stale same-token lifetimes, preserves its original single-flight owner, and discards late previous-scope success/failure without clearing a successor load.",
    "Catalog callback and clock reentry cannot publish or start a refresh after retirement. State is committed only after final generation/currentness checks; diagnostics run after the final writes.",
    "Native model listing checks captured credential immediately before the existing GET. Ordinary provider/account/catalog and file/image behavior remain covered by original suites."
  ],
